From de8dd32c38c7c7c3c60272a4f4cce6ae50ee38ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 09:33:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] runtime/v2: link a bundle's work directory with a junction on
 Windows

`NewBundle` ends by linking the task's working directory into the bundle:

    // symlink workdir
    if err := os.Symlink(work, filepath.Join(b.Path, "work")); err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }

Creating a symlink on Windows requires SeCreateSymbolicLinkPrivilege, which
Administrators hold by default and nobody else holds at all. Go already asks for
the one concession the platform offers -- `os.Symlink` passes
SYMBOLIC_LINK_FLAG_ALLOW_UNPRIVILEGED_CREATE and retries without it
(os/file_windows.go) -- but Windows honours that flag only when Developer Mode
is on, which is a machine-wide setting granting unprivileged symlink creation to
every process run by every user, and is commonly disabled by policy on managed
machines. So on a stock Windows 11 desktop this line fails with
ERROR_PRIVILEGE_NOT_HELD for every task bundle, before any shim is launched, and
an unelevated containerd cannot run a container at all. Measured there:

    New-Item -ItemType SymbolicLink   ->  "Administrator privilege required"
    mklink /J  (a junction)           ->  succeeds

A junction -- a directory mount point -- is the same thing for this purpose and
needs no privilege. Windows has no CreateJunction: a junction is an empty
directory carrying an IO_REPARSE_TAG_MOUNT_POINT reparse point, made with
CreateFile + DeviceIoControl(FSCTL_SET_REPARSE_POINT), which is what
createWorkDirJunction does, with the handle flags the standard library's own
os.TestDirectoryJunction uses.

The junction is tried first and `os.Symlink` is kept as the fallback, rather
than replaced. A junction is a strictly less capable link: its target has to be
a fully qualified path on a local volume, so a `--root` on a UNC share, or any
target ntPath declines to express, cannot be a junction and can be a symlink.
Ordering it this way means every configuration that works today still works, the
unelevated one now works too, and neither failing is reported as one error
naming both causes -- which is the only moment at which "which of the two
constraints did this machine violate" is answerable.

What this must never do is succeed without a link, and that is why a junction
whose reparse point could not be set has its directory removed before the error
is returned. `Bundle.Delete` finds the working directory to remove by reading
that link back, and tolerates a failed read by skipping the removal:

    work, werr := os.Readlink(filepath.Join(b.Path, "work"))
    ...
    if werr == nil { return atomicDelete(work) }

so an ordinary empty directory named `work` would not fail anything; it would
quietly stop working directories from being removed. (`cleanupWorkDirs` reaps
them at the next daemon start, so the loss is bounded by a restart rather than
permanent -- which makes it quieter still, not better.)

`os.Readlink` reads junctions: os/file_windows.go's readReparseLinkHandle
handles IO_REPARSE_TAG_MOUNT_POINT alongside IO_REPARSE_TAG_SYMLINK and passes
the substitute name through normaliseLinkPath, and os.TestReadlink covers
`mklink /J` explicitly. `os.RemoveAll` does not descend into one either: a mount
point is a name-surrogate reparse point, so fileStat.mode() withholds ModeDir
from it exactly as it does from a symlink. The bundle's layout and its teardown
are therefore unchanged; only the reparse tag differs.

Nothing outside Windows changes. The symlink is correct wherever creating one is
unprivileged, and the runc shim resolves <bundle>/work by walking that path
rather than reading it, so !windows keeps calling os.Symlink verbatim.

The buffer encoder lives in worklink.go, deliberately without a build
constraint, so that the one thing here that can be got wrong quietly -- the
REPARSE_DATA_BUFFER layout, where a mis-stated ReparseDataLength earns nothing
but ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER and a swapped name offset earns a junction pointing
somewhere else -- is checked by tests on builders that have no Windows.
